          "title": "You'll want to know: continuō",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "continuō — \"straightaway, without a pause\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puella epistulam lēgit et continuō flēvit. The girl read the letter and straightaway burst into tears."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The adverb of continuus, 'unbroken': nothing comes between. Do not confuse the verb continuō, continuāre, 'to join in a row'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "continuō is the ablative of continuus, \"unbroken\", which is built on contineō, \"to hold together\" (EtymDB). English continue comes from the same family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
