              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "extemplō — \"in an instant, forthwith\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Extemplō portae clausae sunt. In an instant the gates were shut."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Old and lively in tone: Plautus, Vergil and Livy use it. Plautus also has the longer extempulō."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "extemplō comes, says Lewis & Short, from ex and tempulum, a diminutive of templum in its old sense \"a marked-out space\": out of the very moment."
            }
          ]
        },
